source: 52a1e84
date: 2026-08-21 (library 3.0.0 -- FORMAT 3, and 2.9.1/2.9.0/2.8.0/2.7.0/2.6.0
      below it.

      A FORMAT CHANGE IS A RE-VENDOR AND A RE-VERIFY, NOT A PORT -- and that
      claim, which this engine's doc has made since the first vendoring, has
      now been TESTED by a real one rather than merely asserted.  An in-order
      file stopped storing records and started storing
      [header][pointers][keys][values][trailer]: a dense array of key entries
      pointing into an unframed values region.  Not one line of btree_zs.c
      moved for the layout.  The engine never reads a file name, counts files
      or knows the layout, and that is why.

      ONE API CHANGE DID REACH US, and it was not the format.  ZS_NOCSUM is
      GONE (A-18).  No record carries a checksum in format 3 (F-13a) and an
      in-order file's regions are never verified on a read path (F-33a), so
      the flag had nothing left to select; upstream REJECTS the bit rather
      than ignoring it, on the ground that a caller passing it believes it is
      weakening verification and is entitled to be told otherwise.  So our
      `zs_nocsum=1` URI parameter is deleted rather than left as an inert
      alias -- an inert alias would have been the one outcome upstream took
      trouble to prevent.  Nothing in this tree set it.

      WHAT THAT COSTS US, STATED PLAINLY: the read path now verifies NOTHING.
      Region checksums are checked by zs_db_check_consistency and by a repack,
      never by a read, so between consistency checks corrupt bytes in an
      in-order file are returned to us and we hand them to the VDBE.  That is
      upstream's deliberate trade (F-5e), but it is a REDUCTION in what this
      engine offered over the stock btree, and the doc's old pitch -- a safety
      net stock never had -- now has to say the net is cast at maintenance
      time.  Crash safety is untouched: span and terminator checksums keep
      their role in the active file, so a reopen after a crash still yields a
      valid prefix, with or without zs_nosync.  `zs_csum=none` survives as the
      one remaining URI opt-out (it selects engine 0 for files this handle
      WRITES, which is a different lever).

      A FORMAT-2 DATABASE READS AS EMPTY, and there is now a fixture that says
      so.  Formats reject each other at the magic (F-6b, R-6) with no
      migrator, which at this layer surfaces as an empty database rather than
      an error -- format-guard.sh case 4b, built by running zskvbench against
      the 2.9.1 sources, holds a 50-row `kv` table whose CREATE TABLE is still
      legible in the file while the engine reports zero tables.  The second
      assertion exists because the first passes for free on an empty
      directory; both were mutation-checked, and each mutant kills exactly its
      own check.  Nothing has shipped on format 2 so nothing needs carrying
      forward, but any benchmark or test directory left over from before
      2026-08-21 will read empty and the first write will start a fresh
      generation beside data still on disk.

      READS ARE UP, AND UPSTREAM'S NUMBERS REPRODUCE.  Raw zsbench, 500k
      records, 4 passes per arm with the arm order alternated, paired against
      6fe9862 -- format 2 carrying the benchmark fix but not the format, which
      is upstream's own pairing and matters (see below):

          fetch (6 files)             456-473k/s -> 526-548k/s   +11..20%
          fetch (6 files, repacked)   418-441k/s -> 472-486k/s   +7..16%
          full scan                   51.4-52.2M -> 69.0-75.6M   +32..47%
          full scan, compacted        61.2-62.6M -> 90.9-91.7M   +45..50%
          full scan, interleaved      39.1-42.0M -> 50.2-52.6M   +20..35%
          full scan, shadowed         30.4-35.2M -> 43.0-44.9M   +22..48%

      Upstream measures +14% on the repacked fetch and +37% on the compacted
      scan; we land on or above both.  `full scan, no verify` is simply GONE
      as a row -- there is no verification left for it to skip.  The one
      disagreement is `full scan, shadowed`, which upstream reports DOWN 5.4%
      with no mechanism attached and which is UP here; different host, and
      neither of us can explain it, so it stays flagged rather than averaged.

      BULK STORE IS DOWN 10-13% AND THE MECHANISM IS CLOSED.  2M records at
      1000-per-txn, rowid, through SQL, 6 passes per arm, order alternated:
      1726-1746k/s -> 1520-1560k/s.  The instrumented merge counters say why,
      and they are far quieter than the wall clock -- identical BYTES, more
      TIME:

          conversions   117, 263MB, 123-127ms -> 117, 265MB, 170-176ms  +37%
          repacks        40, 830MB, 393-427ms ->  39, 841MB, 497-532ms  +21%
          merge total          516-554ms      ->       667-708ms       +150ms
          the store row's own delta                                    +147ms

      So the extra merge time IS the whole regression, with nothing left over.
      Confirmed underneath us on upstream's own fixture, where the arms are
      quieter still: raw `store, 1000 per txn` 2406-2438k/s -> 2193-2201k/s
      (-9%), `store, all in one txn` 4353-4451k -> 4200-4236k (-3..-5%).

      Upstream priced the CONVERSION half of this and called it not worth
      chasing -- one 20MB conversion going 10.3ms -> 12.5ms, "about 2% of a
      bulk load that seals", against +19% on a scan.  Two things that figure
      misses at our scale, and they are the report to send back: the REPACK
      path pays the same builder cost (+21%, which upstream did not measure),
      and in a cascading bulk load repacks are ~3x the conversion bytes, so
      the weighted cost is 150ms and not 2ms.  The cause upstream names is the
      builder's 32-byte descriptor per record and its second pass, where
      format 2 encoded into one buffer in a single pass.

      Write amplification otherwise IMPROVES: at 200k, WITHOUT ROWID 6.7x ->
      5.6x of stored at 100-per-txn and 6.9x -> 5.8x at 1000, rowid 1000/txn
      2.7x -> 2.3x.  One row goes the other way -- rowid at 100-per-txn, 2.7x
      -> 3.5x -- and it is a cascade BOUNDARY effect, not a per-record cost:
      the same data at 1000-per-txn repacks 3 times for 27MB instead of 4 for
      36MB.  Deterministic per arm across every pass, both directions.

      THIS LAPTOP RESOLVES NOTHING AT 500k THROUGH SQL, and that is worth
      recording as an instrument fact.  Eight passes per arm on the full
      sweep: every row overlaps, including the store row that had
      non-overlapping ranges at three passes.  The 2M result above stands
      because merge work is a large enough share of that run to clear the
      noise; the same question at 500k is unanswerable here.  Read the raw
      rows and the instrumented counters, not the SQL wall clock.

      OUR OWN BENCHMARK CARRIED UPSTREAM'S OVERFLOW BUG.  test/zskvbench.c had
      the identical `(i * 7919) % nrecs` int multiplication that upstream
      fixed in 6fe9862: above n = 271181 roughly half the products wrap and
      the row times a hit/miss MIXTURE under a label claiming the hit path,
      with the miss half the cheaper one.  Fixed here the same way.  It never
      corrupted a published number -- every fetch figure in doc/ is at 20k or
      200k, and prodrun.sh's large-n phases all pass --only, which runs no
      fetch -- but the first `-n 500000` run would have.  It also nearly cost
      this vendoring a wrong conclusion: the first raw fetch A/B, run against
      ffbb7e1 rather than 6fe9862, showed format 3 LOSING, because the format-2
      arm was answering 271183 lookups where format 3 answered 500000.

      Test tier, all green on 3.0.0: zskey-test, zsbtree-test, the 10 SQL
      batteries, format-guard (now 8 cases), crash-test 10 rounds, busy-test,
      backup-concurrent, 966/966 files through the TCL permutation, plus
      twowriter 6/6 and metacache 8/8.

      Also 2.9.1, and 2.9.0/2.8.0/2.7.0/2.6.0 with it.

      THE LOCK PROTOCOL CHANGED and nothing here had to move, but two of our
      own comments were wrong afterwards.

      The remove lock (byte 2) is GONE: every removal is justified by a file
      the remover published beforehand whose range encloses the candidate, and
      enclosure is transitive, so removals commute.  D-23's precondition is
      restated in local form (is THIS candidate enclosed by a file that is not
      itself going away) because a set-wide predicate only looked like it
      raced.  Byte 2 is reserved rather than reused, so a peer still taking it
      interoperates and contends with nobody.

      C-1d REVERSED to write -> repack, asserted in zsi_lock_take.  Upstream
      calls the order interoperability surface and took the reversal with no
      version bump under F-7a, since nothing on disk changes -- a defensible
      call for a pre-release library, and stated rather than quiet.

      Nothing in this engine takes a zeroskip lock directly, so the change is
      invisible to us at runtime: busy-test, backup-concurrent, twowriter and
      crash-test are all clean, run repeatedly because locking is what moved.
      What DID need fixing is a rationale we had copied from upstream's old
      direction, in btree_zs.c and in the doc: "the cascade runs at BEGIN
      because C-1d orders repack before write, so the merge cannot hold the
      write lock."  Under write -> repack a commit could now take the repack
      lock in order, so the structural argument is dead.  BEGIN is still right,
      but for a latency reason -- a merge inside a commit's write lock holds it
      for the merge's duration.

      All three stale sites we reported are fixed in 0e07b3a, and the
      corrected reason is better than the one we had written for ourselves:
      the cascade runs at BEGIN not because a commit cannot take the repack
      lock (it can) but because the cascade is UNBOUNDED (D-16b), so taking
      repack inside a commit would hold the WRITE lock across an unbounded
      merge and block every other writer for its duration.  Our two sites now
      say that.

      0e07b3a is COMMENTS ONLY, verified rather than read off the diff:
      stripping debug info from zeroskip.o built either side gives
      byte-identical objects.  Comparing the unstripped .o says "differs",
      because -g embeds line numbers and a comment shifts them -- which nearly
      got reported here as a code change.

      2.9.1 itself is the doc fixes and the two-thread test: rollover_txns no
      longer claims an index_dir handle is exempt from the span bound (untrue
      since P-13, and it is the field a caller reads to pick a seal cadence),
      and zs_db_seal carries our 500-vs-4000 cadence measurement and the
      cascade-armed interaction.

      ONE OF THOSE FIXES MATTERS TO US AND WE ARE CLEAN ON IT.  rollover_txns
      used to say "A handle with index_dir set is already bounded by P-13 and
      will not reach this", which stopped being true AT P-13 -- a commit no
      longer publishes, so nothing resets the window any more.  A caller
      picking a seal cadence would have read the opposite of the behaviour.
      We never carried that claim: the 2.5.0 entry below records the corrected
      version, and our cliff fixtures pin zs_rollover_txns explicitly rather
      than relying on any exemption.

      zs_db_seal's documentation now carries our 500-vs-4000 measurement and
      the interaction we found (sealing frequently with the cascade ARMED takes
      rewriting 3.2x -> 4.3x of stored), so the three-lever combination is
      stated upstream rather than rediscovered per caller.

      THE WILLNEED HINT IS MEASURED AND IT WORKS (2026-08-20, ZFS 128K, 2M
      records, TWO runs of three alternating passes each): major faults 126109
      -> 122482, -3627 (-2.9%), IDENTICAL TO THE UNIT -- six samples per arm,
      two independent runs, two fresh builds of each arm.  Minor faults rise
      ~612 while major falls 3627, so most of those faults stop happening
      rather than getting cheaper: consistent with readahead plus fault-around
      mapping a run per fault, offered as mechanism and not measured, and the
      magnitude varies fourfold run to run so only the sign is a finding.
      NO TIMING SIGNAL: after one run the arms looked just separable and we
      said the clock corroborated; pooled over twelve samples the hint arm's
      range encloses the other's and holds both the fastest and slowest
      samples.  3015 net faults avoided at 15.9us is 48ms, 2.4% of a 1.99s
      run, against +/-0.5% noise on the quiet arm -- real and below this
      fixture's resolution.  The fault count is the whole result.
      2.9% is a FLOOR: with the ARC warm every fault is a memory hit, and the
      asynchronous-issue benefit that matters on a cold pool is what this
      fixture cannot exercise.  Upstream's condition for deleting the call is
      not met.

      (The first run of this A/B measured NOTHING: prefetch-ab.sh chose its
      without-hint arm by history position, which at 2.9.1 selected the commit
      that introduced the hint, so both arms had it -- 122482 major faults
      identical across six runs, which reads as a clean null and is a null
      instrument.  Arm selection is by posix_madvise call count now.)

      Superseded note -- the hint could not be measured on APFS or
      by any existing fixture here, because they all write the merge input and
      merge it moments later.  test/zs/prefetch-ab.sh is the measurement that
      can see it: build with the cascade disarmed, drop the page cache, then
      time and FAULT-COUNT the merge alone, both libraries interleaved with
      alternating arm order.  Calibrated on APFS, where it correctly reports
      no difference (identical minor-fault counts, zero major).  Upstream's
      position is that if it does not show on ZFS it should come out.

      THE MERGE PATH HINTS THE KERNEL (2.9.0): one
      posix_madvise(POSIX_MADV_WILLNEED) per input in both merge paths, from
      our call graph showing 11.6% of a bulk load in page faults under
      XXH3_hashLong -- the verify pass that opens a conversion or a repack,
      dominated by servicing one fault per page rather than by hashing.
      WILLNEED not SEQUENTIAL deliberately: C-4c shares an immutable file's
      mapping between snapshots, so SEQUENTIAL would change eviction policy
      under a concurrent point-lookup reader.  Advisory and unmeasurable on
      APFS, where the pages are already resident.

      THREAD SAFETY IS NOW IN THE HEADER (G-5), which is what we asked for.  A
      handle is NOT thread-safe -- two threads on one handle is caller error,
      and there is no internal mutex.  A second thread with its OWN handle is
      supported and excludes the first exactly as a second process would
      (C-1j), so a background repack thread blocks or gets ZS_LOCKED, never
      corrupts.  Two gotchas we checked ourselves against and pass: a cursor
      from zs_db_begin_cursor WITHOUT ZS_SHARED holds the write lock for its
      whole life (we never call it -- every cursor comes from
      zs_txn_begin_cursor on a transaction we already own, and read
      transactions pass the shared flag), and locks are ordered within one
      database so a caller holding several needs its own order (C-1h; see
      sqlite3ZsSeal's neighbours in btree_zs.h for where ATTACH leaves us).

      UPSTREAM REWROTE THE SEAL CADENCE ADVICE (ffbb7e1, header comments only;
      verified by stripping debug info -- the objects are byte-identical), and
      measured from their side the mechanism we could only infer from ours.
      Against the two layouts our run produced -- 196 files with no cadence,
      400 with one every 500 commits -- a point lookup is 24% slower on a HIT
      and 40% slower on a MISS, three passes, no overlap.  The miss number is
      the one that applies to us: zsbtWrite probes before every insert of a new
      key, so a probe-then-insert caller pays the worse figure on every commit.
      The header now says a cadence is a file-count knob first and a latency
      knob second, names the disarmed-cascade pairing as the thing not to do,
      and calls seal a bulk-import and maintenance tool rather than a
      steady-state mode.  Their old text prescribed exactly the pairing we then
      measured as a 73% throughput loss, from a fixture of ours too small to
      show it.

      zs_db_seal: RETRACTED ON ZFS.  Our laptop measurement said sealing from
      idle removes the merging-commit class for free; production says it costs
      73% of throughput and 245% of the median, and makes p99.9 WORSE, because
      disarming the cascade lets the file count grow and a probe is linear in
      it (D-14d) -- 400 generations instead of 195.  The 20000-record APFS
      fixture was too small to contain that.  The deployment answer for a
      per-message writer is the plain default; seal and the bounded catch-up
      remain the right tools for a bulk import only.  Upstream's zs_db_seal
      documentation carries our cadence numbers, which are still correct for
      what they measure -- the retraction is of our RECOMMENDATION, not of
      their lever.

      zs_db_seal IS THE CONVERSION LATENCY LEVER, and we had missed it.
      ZS_NOAUTOREPACK moves repacks off the write path and leaves CONVERSIONS
      on it, which is most of the tail: 19 of 25 slow commits survive
      disarming the cascade.  Sealing from idle preempts them.  Exposed as
      sqlite3ZsSeal (btree_zs.h) and measured -- see the doc's latency
      section: seal more often than rollover_txns (1024 by default, and at one
      record per transaction that bound is what seals a generation, not the
      byte bound) and the merging-commit class disappears entirely rather than
      merely shrinking.

      Also 2.8.0, and 2.7.0/2.6.0 with it.

      A LARGE ROLLOVER IS NO LONGER PUNISHED BY THE PRIVATE INDEX (2.8.0).
      Each delta->base merge in the active file's in-memory index is
      O(nbase) with a record decode per comparison, and it ran once per
      fixed 1024 inserts, so a generation of N records cost N/1024 merges
      of O(N) -- quadratic in rollover_size.  The bound is now
      max(1024, nbase/32).  This is the fix for the anomaly this port
      reported for four libraries: "64MB is the worst setting while
      rewriting a quarter of the bytes."  Confirmed here interleaved, 2M
      records at 1000-per-txn, three passes per arm: 64MB ascending
      894-986k/s -> 1.73-1.82M/s (+92%), 64MB random 138-140k -> 217-220k
      (+58%), and the 2MB default inert on both orders (ranges overlap).
      64MB goes from 0.71x of the default to 1.33x, so the ranking inverts.
      No API or format change; nothing in this engine had to move.

      CONFIRMED ON PRODUCTION (2026-08-19, stl-imap-09, ZFS), 2M records at
      1000-per-txn: 64MB ascending 8.07s -> 5.16s at 4K (+56%) and 7.70s ->
      4.70s at 128K (+64%).  64MB is now the BEST rollover on both
      recordsizes, beating the previous champion (16MB + deferred cascade),
      and the sweep is monotonic for the first time.  Deployment note for us:
      D-9d's 1024-span bound makes rollover_size inert for a per-message
      writer, so this is a bulk-load setting, not a steady-state one.

      Also 2.7.0, and 2.6.0 with it.

      ONE GATE PER COMMIT (2.6.0, C-7): the sync between a span's records
      and its terminator is gone, because the terminator's checksum already
      makes a terminator that reaches disk without its data read as absent
      (F-22).  Confirmed here interleaved at 20k records, durable: 1-per-txn
      22.3-22.6k/s -> 39.0k/s (+73%, upstream measures +81%), 1000-per-txn
      2.42-2.55M -> 2.65-2.76M (+8%, upstream +11%).

      CONFIRMED ON PRODUCTION (2026-08-19, stl-imap-09, ZFS): durable
      1-per-txn 5486/s -> 10203/s at 4K (+86%) and 5017 -> 9880 at 128K
      (+97%), against a control that says the attribution is clean -- that
      row was flat on 2.2.0 and 2.5.0 (5486 -> 5507).  I had predicted 6-7%
      from an strace figure that was SYSTEM time, not latency; the gate is
      86us of wall, of which 12us is CPU, and one 86us gate predicts all four
      transaction sizes and both table shapes.  Upstream was right that a
      real ZIL commit shows it best.

      C-7a, which required an engine change: a failed commit is now an
      UNKNOWN outcome, never "it did not happen".  Our
      sqlite3BtreeCommitPhaseTwo inherited the old two-gate assumption by
      leaving iDataVersion alone on error; it now bumps the data version and
      drops the meta cache, so the connection re-reads rather than trusting
      caches built before an outcome it cannot determine.  We do not retry
      the sync -- C-7a forbids reading a second success as evidence.

      The append buffer grows on demand, 64KB to 4MB (2.6.0), so a
      1000-record transaction now stays buffered and is written once with
      its terminator.  Several "64KB chunk" statements in the doc are
      qualified accordingly.

      AN ABORT WITH NOTHING IN THE FILE WRITES NOTHING (2.7.0, C-8b).
      Verified through SQL both sides of the boundary: 300 transactions of
      10 inserted-then-rolled-back rows leave the active file byte-identical
      at 432 bytes, while one rollback of 60000 rows (6.6MB, past the
      ceiling) writes its span and voids it correctly (count 1,
      integrity_check ok).  A rolled-back span is therefore no longer
      something a conforming writer can be required to produce; reading one
      remains mandatory (F-21, F-25) and our reader side is unchanged.  Also
      fixes a latent bug: a failed flush inside zsi_txn_at used to leave a
      transaction committable over a possibly-torn span, and now poisons it.

      Also 2.5.0.  A COMMIT NO LONGER PUBLISHES a pointer
      table (P-13): publishing amortises a replay and the commit path folds
      rather than replaying, so a table written there cost the writer and
      bought it nothing.  Tables still appear, published by whoever built an
      index by replaying -- including the same writer at its own next open.
      Confirmed here, interleaved: a cached 2M-record load at 1000-per-txn
      goes 1.86-1.95s -> 1.47-1.53s (-21%, upstream measures 22%) and is now
      LEVEL with no cache at all, so the table's write-side cost has
      essentially gone at the default rollover.

      One caller-facing consequence upstream flagged: a writer no longer
      moves its own replay window, so with a cache configured rollover_txns
      governs exactly as it does without one.  A 200k-record load at ONE
      record per transaction goes from 12 conversions to 195 and 4 repacks to
      65 -- much more file lifecycle -- and is still faster here (10.06s ->
      8.86s durable, 1.72s -> 1.42s nosync).  On ZFS, where unlink costs
      1.8ms against nothing on APFS, that sign could flip; prodrun.sh's
      `cached` phase measures it rather than assuming.

      Also 2.4.0.  A read-only handle now CREATES the
      ZS_INDEX_LOCAL cache directory instead of running uncached when it is
      absent (P-2b, R-3).  It could always publish into that directory, so
      refusing only the creation meant enabling zs_index=local on a
      read-mostly database did nothing until an unrelated write came along --
      which is the deployment shape we care about, and it silently invalidated
      the open-side half of our break-even calculation.  Verified end to end:
      a cold import leaves no cache, one read-only open leaves
      zeroskip.cache/zeroskip.index-<uuid>-<gen> for the active generation.
      A read-only MOUNT is unaffected; creation fails and the handle continues
      uncached.

      Also 2.3.0.  One code change since 2.2.1: the default
      pointer-table publish threshold now scales with the file it describes
      instead of being an absolute 32KB (A-9).  That absolute value made a
      large generation publish ~2000 times whatever its size, each publish
      rewriting a table proportional to the whole generation -- quadratic
      in generation size, 4.70GB of table writes at a 64MB rollover.
      It also left a small database with a large rollover barely
      publishing and paying for it at every open.  A caller passing a
      non-zero index_threshold is unaffected; we do not.

      This retires a conclusion recorded here: the -42%/-71% cost of the
      pointer table at 16MB/64MB rollover was that default, not evidence
      against pairing a large generation with the table.  Confirmed
      interleaved at 2M records, 1000-per-txn: 64MB + table 4.57-6.29s on
      2.2.1 -> 2.63s on 2.3.0.)
files: zeroskip.c zeroskip.h xxhash.h zsbench.c
reverse-api: ZS_REVERSE ZS_FETCHPREV (ZS_FETCHNEXT inclusive-GE)
open-data: ZS_OPEN_DATA_INITIALIZER gained a field for repack_max_size in
      2.1.1.  We use the initializer rather than a field list, so a new
      field arrives defaulted; keep it that way.
stats: sqlite3ZsStats() in btree_zs.h exposes zs_db_stats (A-17), so a
      benchmark run reports its own write amplification.
repack: ZS_NOAUTOREPACK is reachable as the URI parameter zs_norepack=1,
      with sqlite3ZsRepackCatchUp() to finish the job from idle time.
      zs_rollover=BYTES sets the generation size; rollover_txns is left at
      the library default deliberately (see zsbtEnsureOpen).
